---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Ceiling Fans With Lights",
    "url": "/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Ceiling Fan Light Not Working?",
    "url": "/ceiling-fan-light-not-working/"
  },
  {
    "title": "DC vs AC Ceiling Fans",
    "url": "/dc-vs-ac-ceiling-fan/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Induction vs Gas Cooktop",
    "url": "/induction-vs-gas-cooktop/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Induction Cookware",
    "url": "/best-induction-cookware/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Seal Granite Countertops",
    "url": "/how-to-seal-granite-countertops/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Granite Sealers",
    "url": "/best-granite-sealer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dishwashers",
    "url": "/best-dishwasher/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-17", modified: "2026-10-17" });
const title = "Best Under Cabinet Lighting: 7 Picks Sorted by Power Source";
const description =
  "Best under cabinet lighting, chosen by power source first: plug-in, hardwired, tape and battery picks compared on CRI, color temperature and lumens per foot.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ge-premium-3-color-hd-24",
    "name": "GE Premium 3-Color HD Undercabinet LED Light Fixture 24 in.",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "Plug-In or Direct-Wire LED Bar",
    "description": "The GE Premium 3-Color HD is the bar we would put in most kitchens because it refuses to make you choose your power source up front. GE says it can be plugged in, direct-wired, or both, so a renter can run it off an outlet today and an owner can wire it to a wall switch during a remodel without buying a different fixture. The 24-inch version is rated at 17 watts and 1,110 lumens, which works out to roughly 555 lumens per foot, the brightest per-foot figure among our picks. GE lists 3000K, 4000K and 5000K color options in the one fixture and a CRI of 90, so you can run warm in the evening and cooler for prep, or simply pick the one that matches your ceiling lights. It is listed as dimmable with a dimmer switch, mounts with built-in captive screws, and links to additional GE bars and pucks through linking cables or flush-mount adapters from a single power source. GE lists a five-year warranty. The weak spots are documentation, not hardware: the product page does not explain how the color is selected, does not list a rated life, and does not name compatible dimmers, so check the install guide before buying a dimmer to match.",
    "pros": [
      "Plug in, direct-wire, or both, per GE",
      "Rated 1,110 lumens from 24 inches, about 555 lumens per foot",
      "3000K, 4000K and 5000K options with a listed CRI of 90",
      "Links to more GE bars and pucks from one power source",
      "Five-year warranty listed"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Product page does not name compatible dimmer types",
      "No rated lifespan listed on the GE page",
      "5000K setting is too cool for most warm kitchens"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For most kitchens, the GE Premium 3-Color HD is the under cabinet light to buy: it plugs in or hardwires, is rated at 1,110 lumens and 90 CRI from a 24-inch bar, and lets you pick 3000K, 4000K or 5000K."
  },
  {
    "id": "wac-120v-3-cct-barlight",
    "name": "WAC Lighting 120V 3-CCT Barlight BA-AC24-CS-WT",
    "badge": "Best Hardwired",
    "category": "120V Driverless LED Bar",
    "description": "If you are opening walls anyway, this is the fixture we would ask the electrician to install. WAC's 120V 3-CCT Barlight runs straight off line voltage with what WAC calls ACLED driverless technology, so there is no transformer to hide in a cabinet. The spec sheet lists four lengths: 8 inches at 355 lumens, 18 inches at 600, 24 inches at 910 and 30 inches at 1,075, which puts the 24-inch model around 455 lumens per foot. Color temperature is switchable across 2700K, 3000K and 3500K, which covers the warm range most kitchens want and stops short of the blue-white end. CRI is listed at 90. Dimming is ELV from 100 to 10 percent, so it wants an electronic low-voltage dimmer rather than a basic one. WAC describes a backlit parabolic reflector and an acrylic diffuser with minimal gaps between fixtures for a continuous line, a true one-inch profile, damp-location listing, Title 24 JA8 compliance, a 50,000-hour rated life and a five-year warranty. It only comes in white, and WAC sells interconnect cables and a power cord as separate accessories, so price the full run before committing.",
    "pros": [
      "120V driverless, so no transformer to hide",
      "Switchable 2700K, 3000K or 3500K with a listed CRI of 90",
      "ELV dimming from 100 to 10 percent",
      "Damp-location listed and Title 24 JA8 compliant",
      "Five-year warranty and 50,000-hour rated life"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "White finish only",
      "Interconnects and power cord are sold separately",
      "Needs an ELV-compatible dimmer, not a basic one"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The hardwired bar to specify during a remodel: warm switchable color, 90 CRI, ELV dimming and no driver to hide."
  },
  {
    "id": "armacost-ribbonflex-pro-cob-24v",
    "name": "Armacost Lighting RibbonFlex Pro 24V COB LED Strip Light 3000K",
    "badge": "Best Tape Light",
    "category": "Low-Voltage COB Tape (Driver Required)",
    "description": "Tape light is the answer when you want the light source to disappear completely, and COB tape is the version that does not leave a row of dots reflected in a polished counter. Armacost's RibbonFlex Pro COB strip packs its chips tightly, 97 LEDs per foot per the retailer listing, under a continuous phosphor line. It is rated at 165 lumens per foot and 1.2 watts per foot, with a listed CRI of 90 or higher, and it comes in 2700K, 3000K and 4000K. The roll is 16.4 feet, which is also the listed maximum run, and it can be cut every 2 inches and rejoined with the included connector, so it fits awkward cabinet runs that a fixed-length bar cannot. It is 8 millimeters wide with 3M adhesive backing and support clips, UL listed, IP20 for indoor dry locations, and carries a three-year warranty. It is PWM dimmable with compatible dimmers and controllers. The catch is that it runs on 24 volts DC, so you need a 24-volt power supply sold separately, and ideally an aluminum channel with a diffuser if the strip will be visible. At 165 lumens per foot it is gentler than a bar, so treat it as even ambient and accent light more than heavy task light.",
    "pros": [
      "COB construction for a continuous line instead of dots",
      "Listed CRI of 90+ in 2700K, 3000K or 4000K",
      "Cut every 2 inches to fit any run",
      "16.4-foot roll at a rated 1.2 watts per foot",
      "UL listed with a three-year warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Requires a separate 24V power supply",
      "Rated 165 lumens per foot, softer than a bar fixture",
      "IP20, so not for wet or damp spots"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cleanest-looking option for glossy counters if you are willing to add a 24V driver and a channel."
  },
  {
    "id": "ikea-mittled-countertop-strip",
    "name": "IKEA MITTLED LED Kitchen Countertop Lighting Strip Dimmable",
    "badge": "Best Smart System",
    "category": "Modular Driver-Based Strip with Smart Control",
    "description": "MITTLED is the easiest way to get app and remote control under cabinets without stitching together third-party parts, as long as you accept that it is an IKEA system and buy it from IKEA. The strips come in 12, 15, 18 and 24-inch lengths sized for SEKTION cabinets, and IKEA lists the 15-inch strip at 300 lumens and 4.3 watts, about 240 lumens per foot. Every length is warm white at 2700K with a listed CRI above 90, which is exactly where we would set a kitchen used mostly in the evening. The strip is dimmable with the BILRESA remote, sold separately, and it is part of IKEA's smart home range, connecting to the DIRIGERA hub and the IKEA Home smart app for schedules and grouping. IKEA says it installs without an electrician. What the price tag hides is the parts list: each run needs a TRÅDFRI LED driver and an ANSLUTA power cord, sold separately, plus one VÅGDAL connection cord per group of strips. The product page does not state how many strips one driver can carry, so add up the wattage before buying. Owner reviews on IKEA's page note the warm, yellow-leaning tone and the extra cords needed to connect strips at different heights.",
    "pros": [
      "2700K with a listed CRI above 90",
      "Dimmable with the BILRESA remote, app control via DIRIGERA",
      "Sized for SEKTION cabinets, no electrician needed per IKEA",
      "Four lengths from 12 to 24 inches"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Driver, power cord and connection cords all sold separately",
      "Only one color temperature",
      "Sold through IKEA, so Amazon availability is third-party"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The simplest route to remote and app control under cabinets, if you count the driver and cords in the price."
  },
  {
    "id": "wac-120v-3-cct-puck-kit",
    "name": "WAC Lighting 120V 3-CCT Puck Light Kit HR-AC73",
    "badge": "Best Hardwired Puck",
    "category": "120V Driverless Puck Lights",
    "description": "Pucks make sense in two places: inside glass-front cabinets and under shallow open shelving where a long bar looks heavy. WAC's 120V 3-CCT pucks are the version we would choose because they carry the same driverless ACLED design and specs as the brand's bars. Each puck is rated at 4 watts and 290 lumens with a CRI of 90, and color is switchable across 2700K, 3000K and 3500K. Dimming is ELV from 100 to 10 percent. They can be recessed or surface mounted, and WAC lists up to 20 pucks per run, with a hardwire box, interconnect cable and power cord available as accessories. The HR-AC73 kit bundles two double-wire pucks, one single-wire puck and a cord. Finishes are black, brushed nickel and white, and WAC rates them for 50,000 hours. The trade-off is the physics of any puck: light comes out in pools, so on a long counter you will see bright circles with dimmer gaps between them unless you space them closely.",
    "pros": [
      "290 lumens and a listed CRI of 90 per puck",
      "Switchable 2700K, 3000K or 3500K",
      "Recessed or surface mount, up to 20 per run",
      "Black, brushed nickel or white finishes"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Pools of light rather than an even wash on long counters",
      "Needs an ELV-compatible dimmer"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The puck to buy for glass-front cabinets and open shelving, with the same warm color range and 90 CRI as WAC's bars."
  },
  {
    "id": "energizer-rechargeable-motion-light-bar",
    "name": "Energizer Rechargeable Motion-Sensing Light Bar 81758",
    "badge": "Best Rechargeable",
    "category": "USB-C Rechargeable Motion Bar",
    "description": "This is not countertop task lighting, and we would not pretend otherwise. It is the right tool for the places a wired fixture cannot reach without a contractor: a pantry, the inside of a deep base cabinet, a coffee corner with no outlet. Energizer rates it at 200 lumens total and powers it with a 3.7-volt, 800 mAh lithium polymer battery that charges over the included 1-foot USB-C cable from a USB-A port or adapter, which is not included. A three-position switch selects always on, off, or auto, and in auto the light turns on when it detects movement within 15 feet. It mounts with an included magnetic strip that attaches to most metal surfaces. Energizer's listing does not give a color temperature, CRI or runtime per charge, so if light quality is the point, this is the wrong pick. If the point is never fumbling in a dark cupboard again, it does exactly that with no wiring at all.",
    "pros": [
      "No wiring: USB-C rechargeable battery",
      "Motion activation within a rated 15 feet",
      "On, off and auto modes",
      "Magnetic mounting strip included"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "200 lumens total, not enough for prep work",
      "No color temperature, CRI or runtime listed",
      "USB power adapter not included"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for pantries and cabinet interiors where any light beats none and running a wire is not an option."
  },
  {
    "id": "brilliant-evolution-wireless-puck-brrc134",
    "name": "Brilliant Evolution Wireless LED Puck Light with Remote BRRC134",
    "badge": "Best Battery Puck",
    "category": "AA Battery Puck Lights with IR Remote",
    "description": "For renters who want a warm glow under the cabinets tonight and nothing they cannot peel off later, battery pucks are the honest answer, and Brilliant Evolution's remote-controlled pucks are well documented. The owner's manual lists 55 lumens per puck at 3000K warm white from three LEDs with a 120-degree beam, running on three AA batteries per puck with a rated 100 hours of run time. The infrared remote works up to 15 feet away and one remote can control up to 12 pucks; it sets brightness to 50 or 100 percent, adjusts it up or down, and offers 15, 30, 60 and 120-minute auto-off timers. Each puck can also be turned on or off by pressing the lens. They mount with adhesive tape or screws and measure 3-3/8 inches across and 1-1/4 inches high. Keep expectations in line with the numbers: 55 lumens is accent light, three LEDs will show as bright points on a glossy counter, the remote needs line of sight, and you will be buying AA batteries regularly.",
    "pros": [
      "No wiring at all, adhesive or screw mount",
      "Rated 3000K warm white",
      "IR remote with dimming and 15 to 120-minute timers",
      "One remote controls up to 12 pucks"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "55 lumens per puck is accent light only",
      "Three AA batteries per puck to replace",
      "Infrared remote needs line of sight"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The no-tools option for renters who want warm accent light under cabinets and accept battery swaps."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Under Cabinet Lighting",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-under-cabinet-lighting/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What color temperature is best for under cabinet lighting?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most kitchens, 2700K to 3000K warm white looks best because it matches typical ceiling lighting and flatters wood, cream and warm stone. Choose 4000K if your kitchen is white and gray or you do detailed prep at night and prefer a crisper light. The more important rule is matching your existing overhead lights so the counter does not look like a separate room."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What CRI should under cabinet lights have?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Look for a listed CRI of 90 or higher. CRI describes how accurately a light renders colors, and lower values make food, wood and stone look flat or slightly gray. Every wired pick on this page lists 90 or higher. Treat CRI as a manufacturer rating, and if color accuracy matters to you, install one fixture and judge it at night before buying the full run."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many lumens do I need for under cabinet lighting?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Compare lumens per foot rather than total lumens. For a main prep counter, the bar fixtures on this page land at several hundred lumens per foot, which is the tier we would use for task lighting, ideally on a dimmer. For accent light where overhead lighting is already strong, tape or strip lights in the 150 to 250 lumens per foot range are usually enough."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should under cabinet lights go at the front or back of the cabinet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Toward the front, in most kitchens. Mounting just behind the face frame or a light rail spreads light across the full depth of the counter and keeps the light source out of your line of sight and out of reflections in the counter. Back-mounted lights over-light the backsplash and leave the working edge of the counter in shadow."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need an electrician to install under cabinet lighting?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not for plug-in, battery or rechargeable lights, which are straightforward DIY. Hardwiring a fixture to a circuit, adding a switch or running cable in a wall is electrical work that may require a permit and inspection depending on where you live. If you are not experienced with household wiring, hire a licensed electrician, especially during a remodel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are puck lights or light bars better under cabinets?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Light bars are better for countertops because they produce a continuous, even wash. Puck lights create pools of light with dimmer gaps between them, which looks great inside glass-front cabinets and under open shelving but scalloped on a long counter. If you want pucks over a work surface, space them closely and choose a high-output model."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can under cabinet lights be dimmed and controlled from an app?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many can. Hardwired fixtures like the WAC bars dim with a compatible ELV dimmer, which can itself be a smart dimmer if it is rated for that load. IKEA's MITTLED system dims with its own remote and connects to the IKEA Home app through the DIRIGERA hub. Battery pucks typically offer simple remote dimming and timers rather than app control."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "under cabinet lighting",
      "under cabinet lights",
      "LED under cabinet lighting",
      "under cabinet light bar",
      "under cabinet LED tape light",
      "under cabinet puck lights",
      "kitchen under cabinet lighting"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Under Cabinet Lighting</span> Picks
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Sorted the way the decision actually works: power source first, then shape, color, CRI and placement. Every spec comes from the manufacturer, and we say where one is missing.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ge-premium-3-color-hd-24"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#power-source" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Choose by Power Source</a>
          <a href="#form-factor" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Bar vs Puck vs Tape</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#color-and-cri" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Color Temperature and CRI</a>
          <a href="#brightness" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Lumens per Foot</a>
          <a href="#placement" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Placement and Glare</a>
          <a href="#dimming-and-smart" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Dimming and Smart Control</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Install Notes</a>
          <a href="#match-your-kitchen" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Match a Pick to Your Kitchen</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most under cabinet lighting disappoints for one of three reasons: it was the wrong power type for the kitchen, it was mounted at the back of the cabinet, or it had a color and CRI that made the counter look cheap. Pick the power source first and the rest gets easy.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="power-source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Start With the Power Source, Not the Fixture
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most under cabinet lighting guides start with the fixture and leave power until the end. That is backwards. How the light gets electricity decides what you can buy, whether you need an electrician, how the finished job looks, and what you will be maintaining in five years. Decide this first and the shortlist almost writes itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Plug-in fixtures</strong> run off a standard outlet, usually through a cord that drops down to the backsplash. They are the fastest install and the easiest to undo, and good ones are bright enough for real task lighting. The cost is a visible cord and an outlet you give up. Many plug-in bars, including our top pick, can later be converted to direct wire, which is the best of both worlds if you think you might remodel.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hardwired 120-volt fixtures</strong> connect to a circuit and are usually run from a wall switch. Nothing dangles, nothing occupies an outlet, and the light behaves like part of the kitchen rather than an accessory. This is the right choice during a remodel or when the walls are already open. Newer driverless designs, like the WAC bars and pucks in our picks, skip the transformer entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Low-voltage systems</strong> run on 12 or 24 volts DC from a separate driver, sometimes called a power supply or transformer. Tape light lives here, and so do modular kits like IKEA's MITTLED. They give the most flexible layouts and the slimmest profiles, but you need somewhere to put the driver where it stays ventilated and reachable.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery and rechargeable lights</strong> need no wiring at all. They are the only realistic option in many rentals, and motion-sensing versions are excellent inside pantries and deep cabinets. They are also the dimmest and least consistent category, so they belong in the accent and convenience role, not over the cutting board.
