        <div id="form-factor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Bar, Puck or Tape: What Each One Looks Like on the Counter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you know the power source, the form factor decides what the light actually looks like on the counter. The three shapes produce genuinely different results, and picking the wrong one is the most common reason people are disappointed by a perfectly good fixture.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bars</strong> are the default for kitchens. A rigid aluminum housing with a diffuser spreads light along its length, so a row of bars gives a continuous wash across the counter. They are the brightest option per foot among our picks, at roughly 455 to 555 lumens per foot for the hardwired and plug-in bars, and they are the easiest to mount cleanly with screws. Their weakness is fixed lengths: a 30-inch cabinet run with a 24-inch bar leaves a darker stretch at the end, so measure each cabinet before ordering.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pucks</strong> are small round lights that throw a cone of light. That makes them lovely inside glass-front cabinets and under open shelves, where you want pools of light on displayed objects. Over a long counter the same pools turn into a scalloped pattern of bright circles and dim gaps. If you love the look, space them closely; if you want an even work surface, choose bars or tape instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tape light</strong> is a flexible circuit with LEDs along its length, cut to size and stuck in place. It is the most adaptable and the least visible, especially recessed in an aluminum channel. Older tape shows individual LED dots, both on the strip itself and reflected in shiny countertops. COB tape, which places chips so densely that the light reads as one line, largely solves that problem, which is why our tape pick is a COB product.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful test: stand where you normally prep and look at the counter, not at the cabinet. If what you see reflected is a row of points, you will notice it every day. If it is a soft line, you will forget the light is there, which is the goal.
          </p>
        </div>

