        <div id="color-and-cri" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Color Temperature and CRI: The Two Numbers That Decide How It Looks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Brightness gets the attention, but color temperature and color rendering are what make under cabinet lighting look expensive or cheap. Both numbers are printed on any decent spec sheet, and every pick on this page lists at least one of them except the Energizer bar, which is why it is limited to pantry duty.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Color temperature: match the room, then the task</h3>

          <p>
            Color temperature is measured in kelvin. Around 2700K to 3000K reads as warm white, close to traditional incandescent bulbs, and it flatters wood cabinets, cream tile and warm stone. Around 4000K reads as neutral white, crisper and more clinical, and it suits white-and-gray kitchens and people who do serious prep at night. 5000K and above looks like daylight and tends to feel stark in a home kitchen.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger rule is consistency. Under cabinet lights that are cooler than your ceiling fixtures make the counter look like a separate room. Check the bulbs or fixtures already overhead, including any ceiling fan light kit, and match them. If you are also choosing a fan, our <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">guide to ceiling fans with lights</a> lists the color temperature of each light kit for exactly this reason. Selectable fixtures like the GE and WAC picks let you settle the question after installation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CRI: why 90 is the floor for a kitchen</h3>

          <p>
            The color rendering index describes how accurately a light source shows colors compared with a reference source, on a scale up to 100. Many standard LEDs are rated around 80, and lower color rendering is what makes raw meat look gray, tomatoes look dull and a granite or quartz counter look flatter than it did in the showroom. For a kitchen we treat a listed CRI of 90 as the minimum. GE, WAC, Armacost and IKEA all list 90 or higher on the fixtures we picked.
          </p>

          <p>
            CRI is a manufacturer rating, and owner impressions sometimes differ from it. Reviews of IKEA's strip, for example, describe its 2700K as yellow-leaning. That is partly the color temperature doing exactly what it says, and a good reason to buy one fixture first, install it, and look at it at night before buying the whole run.
          </p>
        </div>

