          <p>
            For a primary prep counter, especially one without strong overhead lighting, we would aim for the bar tier, several hundred lumens per foot, with a dimmer so you can turn it down in the evening. That is what lets you see the edge of a knife and the color of a sauce. If the kitchen already has good recessed lighting and you mainly want a warm glow and a lit backsplash, the strip and tape tier is plenty and often looks better because it is softer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery lights are a separate category entirely. A 55-lumen puck is a nightlight with ambitions, and a 200-lumen motion bar is perfect for finding the cereal at 6 a.m. Neither will light a work surface, and no amount of extra units fixes the uneven pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two caveats keep this honest. First, the per-foot figures here are simple division of manufacturer ratings, not measurements, and the diffuser, mounting depth and color of your counter all change how bright it looks. Second, more is not always better. A very bright fixture mounted toward the back of the cabinet will mostly light the backsplash and bounce glare off a polished counter, which brings us to placement.
          </p>
