        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Placement: Front Edge, Glare and Glossy Stone
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where you mount the fixture matters as much as which fixture you buy. The most common install, tucked against the wall at the back of the cabinet, is also usually the worst. It lights the backsplash brilliantly, leaves the front of the counter where you actually work in relative shadow, and points the light source straight at your eyes in the reflection off the counter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The better default is to mount the fixture toward the front of the cabinet's underside, just behind the face frame or light rail, so the light falls across the full depth of the counter and the backsplash gets a softer graze. Most framed cabinets have a recessed lip at the front that hides a slim bar or tape channel from view when you are standing. If your cabinets are frameless, a light rail molding does the same job and is worth the small cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Glossy counters change the rules. Polished granite, quartz and solid surfaces act like a dark mirror, and anything you can see in that mirror you will notice every day. Individual LED dots, gaps between bars and the bright face of a puck all show up as reflections. This is where diffusers and COB tape earn their place, and where angling or recessing the fixture so its lens cannot be seen from a standing position pays off. If the stone itself needs attention before the lights go in, our <a href="/how-to-seal-granite-countertops/">granite sealing guide</a> covers the job, and it is easier to do with the counter clear and the fixtures not yet mounted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Matte surfaces such as honed stone, butcher block and many laminates are far more forgiving. Here a dotted tape or a row of pucks may be perfectly acceptable, and you can prioritize brightness over diffusion.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE PLACEMENT RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Mount toward the front, aim back, and hide the lens.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you can see the light source from where you stand, or see it reflected in the counter, move it forward, recess it, or add a diffuser. The best under cabinet lighting is noticed only as a well-lit counter.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="dimming-and-smart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Dimming, Linking and Smart Control
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dimming is what turns task lighting into the light you leave on at night, and it is where good fixtures and cheap ones separate. The catch is that dimming is a system: the fixture, the dimmer and sometimes the driver all have to agree.
          </p>

          <p>
            The WAC bars and pucks list ELV dimming from 100 to 10 percent. ELV, or electronic low voltage, refers to a dimmer type that pairs well with many LED electronics, and using a basic dimmer instead is a common cause of flicker and buzzing. GE lists its bar as dimmable with a dimmer switch but does not name compatible models on the product page, so check its install guide. Tape light dims through its driver or a controller between driver and strip, and Armacost lists PWM dimming with compatible dimmers. If you are diagnosing flicker on lights you already own, many of the causes overlap with our <a href="/ceiling-fan-light-not-working/">ceiling fan light troubleshooting guide</a>, especially loose connections and mismatched dimmers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Linking is the second system question. A good under cabinet setup runs several fixtures from one power source and one switch. GE says its Premium bars link to more bars and pucks with linking cables or flush-mount adapters. WAC lists up to 20 of its pucks per run. IKEA's MITTLED needs one VÅGDAL connection cord per group of strips on a TRÅDFRI driver, and the Brilliant Evolution remote controls up to 12 pucks. Before buying, sketch the cabinet runs, note any gaps such as a range hood or window, and count the connectors you need to bridge them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smart control comes in two flavors. A system like MITTLED is smart by design, with the BILRESA remote and the IKEA Home app via the DIRIGERA hub. The other route is to keep the fixture simple and make the wall switch smart. For a hardwired run on its own switch, a smart dimmer that is rated for the fixture's dimming type gives you schedules and voice control without tying the lights to one brand's app. Either way, a schedule that brings the counter up to a low level in the early morning is the single most useful smart feature here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Install Notes: When You Need an Electrician
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plug-in, battery and rechargeable lights are true DIY. Plan the cord route so it drops behind an appliance or into a corner, use the mounting screws rather than tape on fixtures that get warm, and keep cords well away from the cooktop. Whether you cook on gas or induction, as weighed in our <a href="/induction-vs-gas-cooktop/">induction vs gas comparison</a>, the cabinet area beside a range gets hot and splattered, so that is not where to leave a loose cord or a driver.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hardwiring is different. Connecting a fixture to a circuit, adding a switch, or running a new cable inside a wall is electrical work, and in many places it requires a permit and inspection, with rules that vary by city, county and country. If you are not experienced with household wiring, hire a licensed electrician. If you are, the basics still apply: shut off the breaker, confirm the circuit is dead with a tester, use approved junction boxes, and follow the fixture's instructions exactly. Kitchen circuits often have specific code requirements, which is another reason to involve a professional during a remodel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Low-voltage systems sit in between. The tape or strip itself runs on low voltage, but the driver plugs in or is wired to 120 volts. Put the driver somewhere ventilated and accessible, such as on top of a cabinet or inside one with airflow, never buried in insulation or sealed in a wall cavity. Size it with headroom over the total wattage of the run. Armacost's strip is rated at 1.2 watts per foot, so a full 16.4-foot roll is about 20 watts before adding margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, test before you fix anything permanently. Lay the fixtures out, power them, and look at the counter at night from where you stand. Fixing a placement or color mistake at this stage takes five minutes; fixing it after the adhesive has set takes an afternoon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="match-your-kitchen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Matching a Pick to Your Kitchen
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you rent or want a weekend upgrade, start with the GE Premium 3-Color HD as plug-in bars, run them from one outlet with linking cables, and choose 3000K. You get real task lighting and a 90 CRI rating without touching the wiring, and you can take it with you when you move. Add Brilliant Evolution pucks inside a glass-front cabinet if you want accent light without cords.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are remodeling, specify the WAC 120V 3-CCT Barlight on a dedicated switch with an ELV-rated dimmer, and use the matching WAC pucks in glass cabinets so the color and CRI match. Ask the electrician to place the feed toward the front of the cabinet run, not the back.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have glossy stone and hate seeing dots, use Armacost's COB tape in an aluminum channel with a diffuser, mounted at the front lip, and power it with a properly sized 24-volt driver. It is the most work of any option here and gives the cleanest result.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want app and remote control with no electrician and you have IKEA cabinets, MITTLED is the most coherent system. If you just need light in a pantry or a dark corner cabinet, put up a few Energizer motion bars and stop thinking about it. And if induction is part of the plan, our <a href="/best-induction-cookware/">best induction cookware guide</a> covers the other half of a modern kitchen upgrade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the fixture before choosing the power source</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A great hardwired bar is useless to a renter, and a battery puck will never be task lighting. Decide plug-in, hardwired, low-voltage or battery first, based on your outlets, your lease and whether walls are open, then shop only within that category. It removes most of the options on any shopping page in one step.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mounting the light at the back of the cabinet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tucking fixtures against the wall lights the backsplash and leaves the front of the counter, where you chop and read recipes, in shadow. It also puts the light source in the counter's reflection. Mount toward the front lip or behind a light rail and let the light fall back across the whole surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring CRI and color temperature</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bright light with a low CRI makes food and stone look dull, and a 5000K light in a warm kitchen makes the counter feel like an office. Look for a listed CRI of 90 or higher and match the color temperature to your ceiling lights, usually 2700K to 3000K in a warm kitchen.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pairing the wrong dimmer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many LED fixtures specify a dimmer type, such as ELV, and a mismatched dimmer is a common cause of flicker, buzzing and a short dimming range. Check the fixture's spec sheet or install guide for the dimmer type and compatible models before buying the switch, not after the lights start misbehaving.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using dotted tape over glossy stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Inexpensive LED tape shows a row of bright points, and a polished granite or quartz counter reflects every one of them. On glossy surfaces use COB tape, a channel with a diffuser, or a diffused bar fixture. On matte counters the dots matter much less, so you can save money there.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What color temperature is best for under cabinet lighting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most kitchens, 2700K to 3000K warm white looks best because it matches typical ceiling lighting and flatters wood, cream and warm stone. Choose 4000K if your kitchen is white and gray or you do detailed prep at night and prefer a crisper light. The more important rule is matching your existing overhead lights so the counter does not look like a separate room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What CRI should under cabinet lights have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a listed CRI of 90 or higher. CRI describes how accurately a light renders colors, and lower values make food, wood and stone look flat or slightly gray. Every wired pick on this page lists 90 or higher. Treat CRI as a manufacturer rating, and if color accuracy matters to you, install one fixture and judge it at night before buying the full run.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many lumens do I need for under cabinet lighting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Compare lumens per foot rather than total lumens. For a main prep counter, the bar fixtures on this page land at several hundred lumens per foot, which is the tier we would use for task lighting, ideally on a dimmer. For accent light where overhead lighting is already strong, tape or strip lights in the 150 to 250 lumens per foot range are usually enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should under cabinet lights go at the front or back of the cabinet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Toward the front, in most kitchens. Mounting just behind the face frame or a light rail spreads light across the full depth of the counter and keeps the light source out of your line of sight and out of reflections in the counter. Back-mounted lights over-light the backsplash and leave the working edge of the counter in shadow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an electrician to install under cabinet lighting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for plug-in, battery or rechargeable lights, which are straightforward DIY. Hardwiring a fixture to a circuit, adding a switch or running cable in a wall is electrical work that may require a permit and inspection depending on where you live. If you are not experienced with household wiring, hire a licensed electrician, especially during a remodel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are puck lights or light bars better under cabinets?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Light bars are better for countertops because they produce a continuous, even wash. Puck lights create pools of light with dimmer gaps between them, which looks great inside glass-front cabinets and under open shelving but scalloped on a long counter. If you want pucks over a work surface, space them closely and choose a high-output model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can under cabinet lights be dimmed and controlled from an app?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many can. Hardwired fixtures like the WAC bars dim with a compatible ELV dimmer, which can itself be a smart dimmer if it is rated for that load. IKEA's MITTLED system dims with its own remote and connects to the IKEA Home app through the DIRIGERA hub. Battery pucks typically offer simple remote dimming and timers rather than app control.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

